<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</h1><p class="st-meta">945 CE – 1004 CE · capital Aleppo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Arab emirate of Sayf al-Dawla, famous for its wars against Byzantium and for a court that sheltered al-Mutanabbi and al-Farabi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> were a family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-taghlib">Banu Taghlib</a> who rose to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. In 944-945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Ali ibn Abi'l-Hayja</a>, known by his title Sayf al-Dawla, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and, after a settlement with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ikhshidid-emirate">Ikhshidids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, made northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> spent his reign fighting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> on the frontier, winning fame as a champion of holy war despite heavy defeats. His court gathered the poet al-Mutanabbi, his cousin the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-firas-al-hamdani">Abu Firas</a>, and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-farabi">al-Farabi</a>. The dynasty was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'i</a> ruling a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> population and taxed heavily to fund war. In 962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros Phokas</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and in 969-970 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> became a Byzantine tributary.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sad-al-dawla">Sa'd al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-dawla">Sa'id al-Dawla</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was squeezed between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Real power passed to the chamberlain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulu-al-kabir">Lu'lu'</a>, who deposed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> in 1004, beginning <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/luluid-aleppo">Lu'lu'id Aleppo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 945 CE – 1004 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.51%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>945 CE – 1004 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aleppo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sayf al-Dawla, Sa'd al-Dawla, Sa'id al-Dawla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hamdanids of Aleppo, Hamdanid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=974&amp;focus=hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo" aria-label="Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamdanid Emirate of